# Untitled

Dopant-Free, Amorphous–Crystalline Heterophase SnO2 Electron Transport Bilayer Enables $>20\%$ Efficiency in Triple-Cation Perovskite Solar Cells
Improving the ohmic contact and interfacial morphology between an electron transport layer (ETL) and perovskite film is the key to boost the efficiency of planar perovskite solar cells (PSCs). In the current work, an amorphous– crystalline heterophase tin oxide bilayer $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{B i}-\mathsf{S n O}_{2})$ ETL is prepared via a lowtemperature solution process. Compared with the amorphous $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ sol–gel film $(S G.S n O_{2})$ or the crystalline $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ nanoparticle $(N P.S n O_{2})$ counter­ parts, the heterophase Bi- $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETL exhibits improved surface morphology, considerably fewer oxygen defects, and better energy band alignment with the perovskite without sacrificing the optical transmittance. The best PSC device (active area $\approx0.09(m^{2})$ ) based on a Bi- $\cdot\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ ETL is hysteresis-less and achieves an outstanding power conversion efficiency of ${\approx}20.39\%$ , which is one of the highest efficiencies reported for $\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ -triple cation perovskite system based on green antisolvent. More fascinatingly, large-area PSCs (active areas of $\approx3.55(m^{2})$ based on the Bi- $\cdot\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ ETL also achieves an extraordinarily high efficiency of ${\approx}14.93\%$ with negligible hysteresis. The improved device performance of the Bi- $\cdot\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ -based PSC arises predominantly from the improved ohmic contact and suppressed bimolecular recombination at the ETL/perovskite interface. The tailored morphology and energy band structure of the Bi- $\cdot\mathsf{s n O}_{2}$ has enabled the scalable fabrication of highly efficient, hysteresis-less PSCs.
## 1. Introduction
In the past decade, organic–inorganic lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been the main focus of research in both academic institutions and photovoltaic industry because of their excellent optoelectronic properties and low materials and fabrication costs. With the optimization of the fabrication methods and device configurations, laboratory-scale PSCs have achieved a power conversion efficiency (PCE) as high as $25.2\%$ , which is on-par with that of conventional crystalline silicon solar cells.[1–3] As a critical part of device configuration, electron transport layers (ETLs) play an essential role in charge separation, electron transport, and hole-blocking in PSC devices. Numerous research outcomes have demonstrated that the energy band alignment and interfacial charge recombination between the ETL and perovskite layer exert a significant impact on the device performance.[4–7] Therefore, it is pre-requisite to develop an ETL with tailored morphology, optoelectronic properties, and compatible band alignment with perovskite, in order to fabricate high performance PSCs.
In the early stages of research, compactmesoporous heterophase titanium dioxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ was widely used as the ETL in n-i-p structure PSCs due to its excellent electron selectivity.[8–10] However, the high-temperature fabrication route $(>450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , low conductivity, and strong photo-oxidation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ under solar irradiation make it an undesirable ETL for PSCs in the long run.[10]
To alleviate these problems, low-temperature processable $(<185^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ metal oxide (MO) ETLs with fewer photocatalytic effects were introduced, such as zinc oxide $(Z\mathrm{nO})$ ,[11] tungsten trioxide $(\mathbb{W}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ ,[12] niobium oxide $(\mathsf{N b O}_{x})$ ,[13] cerium oxide $(\mathrm{CeO}_{x})$ ,[14] ferric oxide $\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\right)$ ,[15] tin oxide $(\mathrm{{SnO}}_{2})$ ,[16] and silicon dioxide $(\mathrm{SiO}_{2}).^{[17]}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in particular, was extensively investigated due to its high optical transparency, electron mobility $(240\ \mathrm{cm^{2}V^{-1}s^{-1}})$ , charge selectivity, and low-temperature processability.[18–21] More importantly, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ has an inherently compatible energy level alignment with lead-halide perovskite, that is, a conduction band (CB) edge slightly lower than that of the perovskite to facilitate electron extraction and a valence band (VB) edge much lower than that of the perovskite to block the holes.[22] These interesting features altogether make $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ a viable ETL to replace its predecessor, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in PSCs.
Most of the MO-based ETLs, including $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , exhibit an attenuated electron conductivity and carrier diffusion coefficient due to the defect-induced charge trapping-detrapping phenomenon.[23] To improve the inherent electronic and interfacial properties of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs, strategies that include compositional engineering and heteroatom doping have been commonly practiced. There are previous reports detailing the heteroatom doping of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ through the replacement of the $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ ions with different elements, including Li,[24] Mg,[25] Nb,[26] $\mathrm{Zn},^{[27]}~\mathrm{Zr},^{[28]}$ and $\Upsilon^{[29]}$ to enhance its optoelectronic properties and modify its electronic band structure. Each of these doping attempts has yielded a considerable improvement in the PCE of PSCs. Nonetheless, it is widely acknowledged that doping is a double-edged sword, whereby an insufficient dopant concentration yields limited improvement, while a superfluous dopant concentration adversely affects the device performance. It is also highly challenging to precisely control the doping density in the host material and there is a lack of measures to ensure the reproducibility of doping. Furthermore, most of the dopant atoms remain electrically active in the host lattice and they are likely to diffuse out from the host lattice to interfacial boundaries under the influence of voltage bias, thereby deteriorating the stability of the entire PSC device system.
Apart from heteroatom doping, another effective approach to modify the energy band structure and defect-states of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is via surface morphology optimization. The morphology and crystallography of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL are key factors that determine the PSC device performance. A conventional sol–gel based $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film $\left(\mathrm{SG}.\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)$ , prepared via the sol–gel approach, was reported to be amorphous in nature and exhibits severe surface and morphological defects, such as many pinholes and voids that cause inferior device performance with poor reproducibility.[30,31] To address this issue, heterostructured ETLs composed of different binary MOs have been designed. For instance, Lee et al. fabricated planar PSCs on an amorphous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /crystalline $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, resulting in a PCE of $19.8\%$ .[32] Liu et al. reported compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ dual ETLs for PSCs, and achieved a PCE of $18\%$ for their devices.[33] Wang et  al. obtained a PCE of $18\%$ for PSCs based on $\mathrm{ZnO}/$ crystalline $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs.[34] Ma et  al. deposited an inorganic ultrathin nanocrystalline $\mathsf{M g O}$ hole-blocking layer on the anode surface and the resulting $\mathrm{MgO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC yielded a PCE of over $18\%$ .[35] The concept of heterostructured ETLs appears to be the key to boosting the performance of PSCs.
In this work, we introduce a facile, sequential spin-coating method to prepare a dopant-free, amorphous–crystalline heterophase $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ bilayer $\left(\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)$ ETL. The $\mathbf{Bi}{\cdot}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ exhibits improved morphology, quenched defects, and better energy level alignment with the perovskite than the widely used $\mathsf{S G}{\cdot}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ . Basically, the $\mathrm{\Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is the hybrid of the amorphous $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and crystalline $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $\left(\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}\right)$ ), in which the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ functions as a scaffold atop the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film to suppress its surface defects and alter its energy band structure. Therefore, the resulting heterophase Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL simultaneously exhibits the unique features of both amorphous $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and crystalline $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ . The mechanism of the interfacial charge transport and recombination of PSCs employing different types of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs have been systematically investigated to clarify the influence of the ETL-perovskite interfacial morphology on the eventual PSC device performance. The results show that the Bi- $\mathrm{\SnO}_{2}$ ETL can inhibit the undesirable contact between the ITO and the perovskite film more effectively than the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL. Simultaneously, Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL also exhibits a morphology with a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio than that of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film, which provides a larger ETL-perovskite interfacial contact area for electron extraction. As a result, the champion $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC (active area $\approx0.09\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) achieved a PCE of ${\approx}20.39\%$ , which is significantly higher than the PCEs of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ counterparts and rivals with the PCEs of state-of-the-art $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -triple cation PSCs reported in the literature (Table S1, Supporting Information). A further application of the Bi- $\mathrm{\SnO}_{2}$ ETL to a large-area PSC (active area $\approx3.55\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) has successfully boosted its PCE from ${\approx}12.23\%$ up to ${\approx}14.93\%$ . Most importantly, there are negligible hysteresis in both small-area and large-area PSCs based on $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs. The dopant-free, amorphous–crystalline heterophase Bi- $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ is an ideal ETL for the scalable fabrication of PSCs with outstanding performance and efficiency.
## 2. Results and Discussions
The surface topographies of the nanostructured $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films on ITO substrates were characterized using an atomic force microscopy (AFM). The 2D-AFM micrograph in Figure  1a shows that the surface of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film is uniformly covered with a high density of dome-shaped nanostructures (nanodomes), with visible voids among the nanodomes. These nanodomes are irregular in size, have a lateral dimension in the range of $0.1\mathrm{-}1.0~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a vertical dimension of ${\approx}30~\mathrm{nm}$ . Despite the relatively wide lateral diameters of the nanodomes, the surface of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film only has an r.m.s. roughness of ${\approx}7.75$ nm. On the other hand, the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film exhibits an entirely different topography. As presented in Figure  1b, the ${\mathrm{NP}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2}$ film has a highly compact, pinhole-free, and ultrasmooth surface, with an r.m.s. surface roughness of only ${\approx}1.33~\mathrm{nm}$ . This is because the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film is obtained directly from the coalescence of ultrafine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ colloidal nanoparticles. The topography of the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, acquired via the sequential deposition (spincoating) of $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ scaffold, is displayed in Figure  1c. Interestingly, the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film simultaneously exhibits the distinctive features of both parent films, that is, i) the loosely packed, nanodome morphology of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film and ii) densely packed, nanoparticles morphology of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ scaffold. The $\mathrm{\Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film also has a much smoother surface (r.m.s roughness $\approx4.68~\mathrm{nm}$ ) than that of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, owing to the overlaying $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ scaffold. The 3D-AFM topographic mappings of the three $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are provided in Figure S1, Supporting Information. Simultaneously, the highresolution 2D-AFM micrographs in Figure  1d–f confirmed that the ultrafine nanoparticles from the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ scaffold not only overlaid the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanodomes, but also filled up the voids among the nanodomes. Based on the AFM micrographs, the topographies of $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are schematically illustrated. As seen from the 3D illustration in Figure  1g), the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film has an inherently coarse texture due to the irregular dimensions of the nanodomes. Contrarily, the highly uniform size of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ colloidal nanoparticles gives rise to the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film with an extremely smooth and fine-textured surface (Figure  1h). In terms of size, the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticle are extremely fine in comparison with the bulky $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanodomes, thus making it easy for the nanoparticles to infiltrate the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film by either filling in the voids among the nanodomes or attaching directly onto the surface of the nanodomes. This yields a $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film which exhibits a medium-textured surface with lower roughness with respect to the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film (Figure  1i). The effective thicknesses (measured via surface profilometer) of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ films were similar $(\approx30~\mathrm{nm})$ , whereas the thickness of Bi- $.\mathrm{{SnO}}_{2}$ film was relatively higher $(\approx40~\mathrm{nm})$ .
The morphology of $\operatorname{SG}{\mathrm{-}}\operatorname{SnO}_{2}$ film is further verified via a field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM). Figure 2a confirms that the surface of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film is composed of uniformly distributed nanodomes which are irregular in size, with visible voids among the nanodomes. In contrast, the surface of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film is composed of highly compact nanoparticles that yields a void-free morphology (Figure S2,
Supporting Information). These findings agree well with the AFM results. Concurrently, X-ray diffraction (XRD) characterization reveals that the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film is amorphous in nature with no obvious diffraction peak (Figure  2b). This finding is already anticipated because typically, an annealing temperature of ${>}450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is required to obtain $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ crystallites that yields prominent XRD diffraction peaks. The post-annealing of the $\mathsf{S G}{\cdot}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ film at $190~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was only intended to accelerate the decomposition of the unstable $\mathrm{\SnCl_{4}}$ phase into the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ phase, instead of promoting crystallization. Meanwhile, broad diffraction peaks were detected in the XRD pattern of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film, arising primarily from the presence of colloidal nanoparticles in the film. The diffraction peaks were broad and subtle due to the finite size of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ crystallites.[36] This phenomenon is known as Debye–Scherrer broadening. The XRD pattern of the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film is rather similar with that of the ${\mathrm{NP}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2}$ film because it also partially contains $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ colloidal nanoparticles. The subtle diffraction peaks in the XRD patterns of both the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films at $2\theta$ angles of $26.6^{\circ},~33.9^{\circ}$ , $38.0^{\circ}$ , $51.8^{\circ}$ , and $54.8^{\circ}$ can be indexed to the (110), (101), (200), (211), and (220) lattice planes, respectively. These peaks indicated that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles scaffold exhibit a cassiterite crystal phase with a tetragonal rutile structure (JCPDS card no.: 01-072-1147).[37] To provide a more in-depth analysis of the crystallography of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ colloidal nanoparticles, high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) characterization was conducted. The HRTEM micrograph in Figure  2c illustrates the quasi-spherical morphology of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles with a diameter of $4{\ensuremath{-}}5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The lattice fringes of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles are present at two different d-spacing values, that is, 3.34 and $\bar{2.56\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ , which correspond to the (110) and (101) planes of cassiterite $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , respectively. Concurrently, the selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanoparticles in Figure  2c demonstrates a set of diffraction rings, indicating the polycrystalline nature of the nanoparticles. The diffraction rings can be indexed to the (110), (101), (211), and (301) crystal planes of the cassiterite $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ crystal, which agrees well with the previous XRD findings. Based on the XRD and HRTEM findings, it is conclusive that the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film has an amorphous phase, the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ film has a crystalline phase, and the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film has an amorphous–crystalline heterophase. Furthermore, the optical transmittance of each $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film was also investigated and the transmittance spectra of the films are presented in Figure  2d. Interestingly, the transmittance of the Bi- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{snO}_{2}$ film was not diminished by the insertion of the NP scaffold or its higher thickness. Instead, the Bi- $.\mathrm{{SnO}}_{2}$ film exhibited the highest transmittance in the UV-range, which is likely due to the stronger light propagation and photon oscillation at the $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ interface with increasing film thickness.[38] To further verify this, a simple finite-difference time-domain simulation has been conducted to calculate the optical transmittance spectra of the nanostructured $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and Bi- $.\mathrm{{SnO}}_{2}$ films on ITO substrate. The detailed simulation method and input parameters can be found in Supporting Information. The simulation results (Figure S3, Supporting Information) suggest that all $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films have lower refractive indices than that of the ITO based on volume averaging theory[39] and act as antireflective layers that lead to enhanced optical transmittance. The simulation results are well in line with the experimental results, confirming the enhanced optical performance of $\mathrm{ITO}/$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films from that of the bare ITO. High transmittance is essential for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films to ensure maximum light harvesting in the perovskite film. From the Tauc Plot (inset) derived from the absorption spectra of the glass/ $\mathrm{\DeltaSnO}_{2}$ films, the optical band gaps $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{\Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films were found to be ${\approx}4.02~\mathrm{eV},{\approx}3.89~\mathrm{eV},$ and ${\approx}3.93\ \mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. The noticeable deviation in $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ implies that there is a significant alteration in the energy band structure of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films following surface modification.
Besides surface morphology and crystallography changes, XPS characterization was conducted to investigate the elemental composition of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and Bi- $S\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ films. The most noticeable difference in the XPS survey scan spectra (Figure S4, Supporting Information) is the detection of the Cl element in the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film which originates from the use of $\mathrm{SnCl}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ precursor. For a more in-depth analysis, resolutions of the $\mathrm{Sn}3\mathrm{d}$ and O 1s spectra for each $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film are provided. There are two pronounced peaks observed in the $\mathrm{sn}$ 3d spectra of all $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films, namely Sn $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Sn $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ peaks located at a binding energy (B.E.) of ${\approx}487.3\ 3$ and ${\approx}495.7\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure S5, Supporting Information). No visible changes were observed in the $\mathrm{Sn}\ 3\mathrm{d}$ spectra despite the morphology and crystallographic changes in the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films. However, substantial changes were observed in the O 1s spectra. As displayed in Figure 3a, the broad O 1s spectra of all $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films can be deconvoluted into three Gaussian subpeaks, denoted as $\mathrm{O}_{1}$ $_1\ (\approx530\ \mathrm{eV})$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(\approx531.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and $\mathrm{~O}_{3}$ $(\approx532.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ , respectively. The detailed atomic percentages of the deconvoluted O 1s components are presented in Table S2, Supporting Information. Specifically, the dominant $\mathrm{O}_{1}$ peak located at a lower B.E. can be associated with the O species in the $S\mathrm{n-O}$ bond, whereas the subsidiary $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{~O}_{3}$ peaks located at higher binding energies are commonly assigned to oxygen vacancies $(V_{0})$ and dangling hydroxyl $(\mathrm{-OH})$ groups bounded on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface, respectively.[40,41] Based on the area under the curve of each O component, the fractions of lattice oxygen atoms $(O_{\mathrm{lattice}})$ and oxygen defects $(O_{\mathrm{defect}})$ in each $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film were calculated. According to Table S2, Supporting Information, the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film exhibited the highest $[O_{\mathrm{defect}}/O_{\mathrm{lattice}}]$ fraction of ${\approx}0.6592$ , indicating the abundance of oxygen-related defects in the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film surface acquired via the sol–gel method. On the contrary, the $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film exhibited the lowest fraction of $[O_{\mathrm{defect}}/O_{\mathrm{lattice}}]$ fraction of ${\approx}0.4596$ , signifying that there are fewer oxygen defects in the film and that the O component arises predominantly from the $S\mathrm{n-O}$ bond. The considerably lower $[O_{\mathrm{defect}}/O_{\mathrm{lattice}}]$ of the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film originates mainly from the reduction of the surface $-\mathrm{OH}$ group $(\mathrm{O}_{3}$ component). These surface $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups and $V_{\mathrm{O}}$ defects could turn into charge recombination centers that hinder the charge carrier transport. Therefore, the significantly fewer O-defects in the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film is highly beneficial for its role as an ETL in PSCs. It is noteworthy that an additional broad shake-up/satellite peak (circled region) was observed in the C 1s spectra of both the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films (Figure 3d). This satellite peak can be attributed to the presence of the carboxyl group $({\mathrm{-COOH}})$ in the ligands of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NPs that caused the sudden emission of photoelectrons during measurement. This particular peak was not observed in the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film due to the absence of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ NPs (ligand host). This finding correlates well with the detection of the additional carboxylic dangling $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond $(1652~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{O-H}$ bond $(3381~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ in the FT-IR spectra of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films (Figure S6, Supporting Information).[42,43]
Concurrently, UPS measurements were conducted to investigate the quasi-Fermi-level alignments of all $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films. Figure  3b,c present the UPS spectra of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films in the Fermi edge region and cut-off energy region, respectively. The figures reveal significant alterations in the $E_{\mathrm{cut-off}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{on-set}}$ parameters of the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films and the detailed values of $E_{\mathrm{cut-off}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{on-set}}$ of each $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film are summarized in Table  S3, Supporting Information. Based on Equation (S1),
Supporting Information, the valance band energy levels $(E_{\mathrm{VB}})$ of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films were determined to be $-8.09$ , -7.56, and −7.77  eV, respectively. Based on Equation  (S2), Supporting Information, the quasi-Fermi energy levels $\left(E_{\mathrm{F}}\right)$ of $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films were found to be −4.45, $-3.77$ , and $-4.06\ \mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. Apparently, there is an upshift in the $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ of the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film with respect to the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, owing to the presence of the overlaying NP scaffold. Based on the value of the $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ obtained from the UPS spectra and the value of $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ derived from the Tauc plot, the energy band diagrams of the three different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are schematically illustrated in Figure  3e. The XPS and UPS results have proven that substantial changes have occurred in the chemical composition and energy band structure of the $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, which can lead to considerable changes in the device performance of PSC.
Next, the photovoltaic performances of the triple-cation PSCs based on $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs were investigated. The device architecture of the PSC is schematically illustrated in Figure 4a. The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the best PSC devices based on the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs are displayed in Figure $\ensuremath{4\mathrm{b}}$ and the detailed photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table  1. The use of $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ ETLs have yielded comparable device performance in the PSCs, achieving an optimum PCE of $\approx18.67\%$ and ${\approx}18.49\%$ , respectively (Table 1). Fascinatingly, the use of the tailored Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL has contributed to significant $V_{\mathrm{oc}},\:J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ and FF improvements, thereby elevating the PCE of the PSC up to a maximum of ${\approx}20.39\%$ . The average photovoltaic parameters of the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -PSC devices are summarized in Table S4, Supporting Information. The corresponding $J{-}V$ curves under both reverse- and forward-scan directions for each of the PSC devices are displayed in Figure S7, Supporting Information, and the hysteresis index (HI) for each PSC was calculated using Equation (S3), Supporting Information. Apparently, the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC exhibited a much lower HI $_{(\approx0.009)}$ than those of the $\operatorname{SG}{\cdot}\operatorname{SnO}_{2}$ $_{(\approx0.017)}$ and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ $\scriptstyle(\approx0.020)$ counterparts, which may be ascribed to the improved ETL–perovskite interfacial morphology.
Simultaneously, the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -PSC devices are displayed in Figure  4c. The integrated photocurrent density values derived from the EQE spectra are in good accordance with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values obtained from the $J{-}V$ measurements (Table S5, Supporting Information). The improved performance of the PSC based on the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL can be closely associated with the better compatibility of its energy band alignment with that of the triple-cation perovskite film, as depicted in Figure  4d. To explain further, the conduction band level $(E_{\mathrm{CB}})$ of $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is higher than that of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL and nearer to the $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}$ of the perovskite film, thus facilitating the extraction of electrons. Similarly, the $\scriptstyle{E_{\mathrm{VB}}}$ of the $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is deeper than that of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ ETL and can, therefore, efficiently prevent the backflow of holes or bimolecular recombination at the ETL/perovskite interface. To further investigate the carrier trap dynamics at ETL/perovskite interface, space-charge-limited current (SCLC) measurements were conducted. For SCLC measurement, electron-only devices with a device architecture of $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite $(500\ \mathrm{\nm})/\mathrm{PCBM/Ag}$ were fabricated to accurately determine the charge trap densities. The dark $J{-}V$ characteristic curves of the corresponding SCLC devices are displayed in Figure 4e. From the SCLC plot, the trap density $(N_{\mathrm{trap}})$ and the trap-filled limited voltage $(V_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ can be calculated using Mott–Gurney relation (Equation (S4), Supporting Information). The $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ values fitted for $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ based SCLC devices were 0.83, 0.69, and $0.81~\mathrm{V},$ respectively. The trap density is directly proportional to the $V_{\mathrm{TFL}},$ therefore, a lower $V_{\mathrm{TFL}}$ signifies a lower concentration of trap states. Correspondingly, the calculated $N_{\mathrm{trap}}$ values for $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ -, $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2^{-}}$ , and $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based SCLC devices are $9.2\times10^{16}$ , $7.3\times10^{16}$ , and $7.7\times10^{16}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , respectively. The ${\mathrm{NP}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2}$ device exhibited the lowest trap density, which is attributable to the compact and smooth morphology of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ ETL. Similarly, the trap density of the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device is also much lower than that of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device. This phenomenon can be ascribed to the presence of the nanoparticles scaffold in the tailored bilayer structure of Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL which induced defect-passivation and helped to the roughness of the film surface. The overall $N_{\mathrm{trap}}$ trend of the SCLC device is in good correlation with the concentration of O-defects revealed in the XPS result and the PL quenching trend. Low trap density is highly desired in $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL to enable efficient carrier transport in the PSC device. On another note, the PCEs of 45 PSCs based on the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs were statistically analyzed and presented in Figure 4f. The whisker plot demonstrates that the device performance for all $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -PSCs are highly consistent and reproducible. The PCEs of $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ devices are generally ${\approx}1.5{-}2\%$ higher than those of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ counterparts.
The device performance of the PSC is closely associated with morphology of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs. Based on the morphology of each $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film, the ETL/perovskite interfacial morphology of PSCs based on different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films are schematically illustrated in Figure 5. In the case of the amorphous $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL (Figure 5a), the formation of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ nanodomes with a high surface-to-volume ratio is beneficial for providing larger $\mathrm{ETL}/$ perovskite interfacial contact area. This implies that there are more active areas available for charge transfer, which is advantageous for the device performance. Nonetheless, the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL suffers from a coarse-textured surface and high void density, which may trigger the unwanted collection of holes by the ITO electrode, especially in the void areas where there is a direct contact between the perovskite film and ITO electrode. On the other hand, the crystalline $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ ETL exhibits an ultrasmooth surface and compact morphology, which could effectively provide a direct electron transport pathway and prevent a direct contact between the perovskite film and the ITO electrode, as illustrated in Figure 5b. However, the fine-grained texture of the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ ETL yields only a limited ETL-perovskite interfacial contact area in comparison with the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, leading to less efficient current extraction. From here, it is deducible that the use of the amorphous $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL is likely to yield a higher carrier (electron) concentration due to the larger ETL-perovskite contact, whereas the use of the crystalline $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ ETL is beneficial for carrier (electron) mobility because of the direct charge transport pathway. In this regard, the design of the amorphous–crystalline heterophase $\mathrm{\Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, which retains the morphological features of the individual $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and NP- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs, appears to be the most practical solution. Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL provides a much larger $\mathrm{ETL}/$ perovskite interfacial area for charge extraction and shields the hole carriers from reaching the ITO electrode more effectively (Figure  5c). This makes $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ an overall better ETL candidate than both of its predecessors. The higher PCE observed in the Bi- $S\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ -based PSC is ascribable to the improved its $\mathrm{ETL}/$ perovskite interfacial morphology.
In conjunction with this, the crystallinity and morphology of the Bi- $S_{\mathrm{nO}_{2}}/$ perovskite film were studied. The XRD patterns in Figure  6a shows that the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite film is highly crystalline in nature and exhibits a dominant (110) peak at a $2\theta$ angle of ${\approx}14.1^{\circ}$ . Besides the (110) peak, the other characteristic peaks of triple-cation perovskite, such as (012), (003), (220), (310), (114), (224), and (314) have also been detected. There is also a noticeable peak at the $2\theta$ angle of ${\approx}12.7^{\circ}$ which reflects the presence of the remnant $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in the perovskite film. The top-view FESEM micrograph in Figure  6b depicts that the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite film is highly compact, pinhole-free and composed of distinguishable grains with an average grain size of ${\approx}389$ nm (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Based on the cross-section FESEM micrograph in Figure  6c, the thicknesses of the vertically-stacked $\mathrm{ITO/Bi{\cdot}S n O_{2}}_{I}$ /perovskite layers are $150\ \mathrm{nm}/40\ \mathrm{nm}/720\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Additionally, a parallel comparison of the XRD patterns and top-view FESEM images of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2/}$ perovskite and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite samples are presented in Figure S9, Supporting Information. Despite the underlaying $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs exhibit distinctively different surface morphologies, there are only negligible changes observed in the morphology and crystallinity of the overlaying perovskite films because the fabrication route of the perovskite films was the same. Apparently, the morphology of the underlaying $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL has no significant influence on the subsequent perovskite film deposition. Next, the optical absorption of the perovskite films deposited on top of the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs were investigated. There are limited changes in the optical absorption of the perovskite films, suggesting that the thickness and light-harvesting efficiency of the perovskite films on the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs are the same (Figure  6d). In parallel with this, a steady-state PL measurement (inset) was conducted to investigate the interfacial charge transfer dynamics of the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ perovskite samples. In the presence of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , the PL emission of the perovskite film quenched significantly as a result of charge transfer at the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2/}$ perovskite interface. Particularly, the PL emission of the perovskite film quenched the most with the use of the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , indicating more efficient electron extraction at the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ perovskite interface. This improvement can be attributed to the fewer oxygen defects and surface $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups in the $\mathrm{\Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, which could turn into deep-level trap states that trigger undesirable carrier recombination. Transient photovoltage (TPV) measurements were conducted to probe the carrier recombination kinetics of and carrier recombination lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{rec}})$ of the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ - based PSC devices. The resulting TPV decay profiles are presented in Figure  6e. Apparently, the $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device exhibits slower TPV decay and significantly longer $\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}$ $_\textrm{c}(\approx22.8~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ than the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $'\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}\approx15.5~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ and ${\mathrm{NP}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2}$ ( $\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}\approx18.1\upmu\mathrm{s}$ counterparts. The prolonged $\tau_{\mathrm{rec}}$ evidences the lesser bimolecular recombination of charge carriers in the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device, which explained its higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ compared to the other devices. To further study the charge transfer dynamics of the PSC devices based on the different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs, an electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measurement was conducted under dark condition, applying a $0.9{\mathrm{~V~}}$ bias at room temperature. As displayed in Figure  6f, the Nyquist plots of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ - and $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}.$ based devices show only one visible arc, whereas the Nyquist plot of Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ comprises two visible arcs. Specifically, the arc at the higher frequency represents the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ , whereas the arcs at the lower frequency is associated with the charge recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ at the interface of the ETL/perovskite that is inversely proportional to the recombination rate of charge carriers. The equivalent electrical circuits for $\mathrm{SG{-}S n O_{2}/N P{-}S n O_{2}}$ -based devices and Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based devices are illustrated in insets of Figure 6a,b, respectively. Most noticeably, the electrical equivalent circuit of $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device consists of two $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ components, suggesting the existence of an additional charge transfer interface arising from the $\mathrm{SG{-}S n O_{2}/N P{-}S n O_{2}}$ bilayer architecture. This additional charge compound resistance is not observed in $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ devices with single layer architecture. The fitted $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values for $\mathsf{S G}{\cdot}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}{\cdot}$ , ${\mathrm{NP}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{2} $ -, and $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ - based devices are 2.32, 3.08, and $5.09\ \mathrm{k}\Omega$ , respectively. The rest of the fitted parameters can be found in Table S6, Supporting Information. In the case of $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , the addition of NPs scaffold on top of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer has effectively reduced the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ and increased the $R_{\mathrm{rec}},$ indicating more efficient charge extraction, transfer, and less interfacial recombination in the device. All of these factors have substantiated the efficiency improvement in the Bi- $S\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ device.
As aforementioned, the use of different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs have yielded PSC devices with different hysteresis behavior. It has been reported that the hysteresis of the PSC device is in close association with the device stability.[44,45] To validate this finding, the long-term stabilities of the non-encapsulated and encapsulated PSC devices based on different $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs were evaluated, and the resulting stability curves are displayed in Figure S10, Supporting Information. In non-encapsulated state, the Bi- $S\mathrm{nO}_{2}$ -based PSC device which exhibits the lowest hysteresis, is displaying improved long-term stability than the $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ counterpart which suffers from more severe hysteresis. Meanwhile, in encapsulated state, all of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -PSC devices displayed $590\%$ PCE retention after ${>}2000$ h of aging in air. Therefore, it is clear that the tailored Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ bilayer is a more ideal ETL candidate because it yields hysteresis-free PSC with great efficiency and stability.
Furthermore, we also fabricated large-area PSC devices with active areas (A) of 1.15 and $3.55~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . The surface of the largearea perovskite film exhibits relatively more pinholes and grain boundaries than the small-area (standard) perovskite film (Figure S11, Supporting Information). As a compensation for the increase of series resistance among the charge transport layers, the perovskite absorber layer and the electrode with the increase of cell size, Ag paste subsidiary electrodes were applied on top of the ITO electrode to ensure efficient current collection, as illustrated in Figure  7a. The $J{-}V$ curves of the large-area ( $\mathrm{1.15~cm^{2}}$ ; $3.55~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ PSCs based on $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ are displayed in Figure  7b and $\mathrm{7c}$ , respectively. Based on the results in Table  2, it is apparent that the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC exhibits considerably greater photovoltaic performance than the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC across different active areas. As a comparison, the largearea $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device shows a remarkable PCE of $14.93\%$ at an active area of $3.55~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , whereas the large-area $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device has a much lower PCE of $12.23\%$ (Figure 7d). Numerically, with increasing A from 0.09 to $3.55~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC shows a PCE retention of ${\approx}65\%$ , whereas the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ based PSC shows a PCE retention of $\approx73\%$ . One of the factors that contributes to the better device performance of the large-area $\mathrm{\Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ device is that there are fewer pinholes in the Bi- $\mathrm{\SnO}_{2}$ ETL due to the presence of the subsidiary NP scaffold layer that fills in the void between the nanodomes, thus effectively reducing the leakage current in the device and leading to a much higher FF. In addition, the surface roughness of the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ is also lower than that of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and it is a decisive factor in enabling a more ideal ohmic contact between the perovskite and the ETL, especially for a large-area device. Besides uplifting device performance, an improved ETL/perovskite interfacial morphology also plays an equally significant role in suppressing the hysteresis of the large-area PSCs. As displayed in Figure S12, Supporting Information, large-area PSCs based on Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ exhibits negligible hysteresis compared to the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ counterparts. At an A of ${\mathrm{\stackrel{.}{\approx}}}3.55~{\mathrm{cm}}^{2}$ , large-area PSC based on Bi- $\mathrm{\SnO}_{2}$ ETL only showed HI of ${\approx}0.017$ , whereas the $\mathbf{S}\mathbf{G}{\cdot}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}2 $ device recorded a much higher HI of ${\approx}0.058$ , indicating much lesser charge trapping and de-trapping due to deep level defect states at the ETL-perovskite interface. Consequently, the PCE disparity between the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}.$ and Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSCs increased systematically with the device active area, from ${\approx}9.2\%$ $(A\approx0.09\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ of the small-area device to ${\approx}18.2\%$ $(A\approx1.15~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and ${\approx}22.1\%$ $(A\approx3.55\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ of the largearea device, as shown in Table S7, Supporting Information.
## 3. Conclusion
This work demonstrates a facile solution route for the fabrication of amorphous–crystalline heterophase $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ as a highly efficient ETL for triple-cation PSCs. Compared with the amorphous $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ or the crystalline $\mathrm{NP-SnO}_{2}$ films, the designed $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film exhibited noticeably improved morphology, crystallinity, and optical properties. The void-free morphology, suppressed oxygen defects, and highly compatible energy band alignment of $\mathrm{Bi}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ with that of the perovskite film enabled more efficient electron extraction at the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -perovskite interface, thereby boosting the PCE of the PSC from $\approx18\%$ to more than $20\%$ . The best Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -based PSC achieved a PCE of ${\approx}20.39\%$ , which is among the highest PCEs obtained for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -triple cation PSC systems in the literature. More fascinatingly, a large-area PSC $(A\approx3.55~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ) based on $\mathrm{Bi}–\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL has recorded a PCE of ${\approx}14.93\%$ , which is an astounding ${\approx}25\%$ improvement from that of the $\mathrm{SG}{\cdot}\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ counterpart. This finding demonstrates that Bi- $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{S}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{n}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{O}}_{2}$ is a much better ETL than both of its predecessors, especially for the fabrication of a large-area PSC. The low-temperature preparation route of the Bi- $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ PSC holds great promise for applications in the roll-toroll manufacturing of flexible, large-area PSCs.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: All of the materials, tin(II) chloride dihydrate $(\mathsf{S n C l}_{2}.2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , $98\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), lead(II) iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , $99.99\%$ , TCI Chemicals), formamidinium iodide (FAI, GreatCell Solar), lead bromide $({\mathsf{P b}}{\mathsf{B r}}_{2}$ $598\%$ , TCI Chemicals), methylammonium bromide (MABr, GreatCell Solar), methylammonium chloride (MACl, Merck), cesium iodide (CsI beads, $99.99\%$ , TCI Chemicals), spiro-oMeTAD $(>99\%$ , Lumtech), bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TSFI, $99.95\%$ , SigmaAldrich), DMF $(99.8\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), dimethyldimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.8\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), ethyl acetate $(99.5\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), hexane $(99.5\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), chlorobenzene $(99.9\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), ethanol $(99.5\%$ , Merck), 4-tert-butylpyridine (tBP, $96\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich), and acetonitrile $(99.8\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich) were used as received without further purification.
Fabrication of Different $S n O_{2}$ -Based ETLs: Patterned ITO coated glass substrates $(\approx10\Omega\sqcap\sqcap^{-1})$ with dimensions of $2.5\ c m\times2.5\ c m$ were ultrasonically washed in acetone, ethanol, and IPA for 10  min each in sequence and then subjected to UV-ozone treatment for $20~\mathsf{m i n}$ Three different kinds of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETLs were prepared in this study, namely amorphous $S G\cdot S n O_{2}$ , crystalline ${\mathsf{N P-S n O}}_{2}$ , and an amorphous– crystalline B $\mathsf{i}{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ . The $S C_{2}.S n O_{2}$ ETL was prepared by spin-coating a precursor solution containing $\mathsf{S n C l}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ in ethanol $(23\mathrm{~mg\mL^{-1}})$ at $3000~\mathsf{r p m}$ for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , followed by annealing treatment at $\mathsf{l}90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in air for an hour. The ${\mathsf{N P-S n O}}_{2}$ ETL was prepared by spin-coating the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ colloidal stock solution (diluted to $2.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in ultrapure $H_{2}O)$ at 3000  rpm for 30s, followed by annealing in air at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ . For the fabrication of the Bi- $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETL, $S G\mathrm{.}S_{n}O_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N P-S n O}}_{2}$ were sequentially deposited using the aforementioned spin-coating and annealing condition. The spin-coating speed of $S C_{2}.S n O_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N P-S n O}}_{2}$ primarily dictated the thickness and subsequent PSC device performance of $B i\cdot S n O_{2}$ . Herein, the spin-coating speed of $S C{\cdot}S n O_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N P-S n O}}_{2}$ was fixed at 3000 rpm in the fabrication of $\mathsf{B i}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETL because it yielded the optimum device performance, as summarized in Figure S13 and Table S8, Supporting Information. The $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ -coated substrates were then transferred into an ${\sf N}_{2}$ glovebox for further perovskite deposition.
PSC Device Fabrication: The planar PSC devices had a device configuration of $170/{\mathsf{S n O}}_{2}/$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag. The triplecation perovskite precursor solution with a general chemical formula of $\mathsf{C s}_{0.05}(\mathsf{F A P b l_{3}})_{0.85}(\mathsf{M A P b B r_{3}})_{0.15}$ was prepared by mixing $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(530\ m g)$ , FAI $(789~\mathsf{m g})$ , $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ ( $75~\mathsf{m g})$ , MABr $(78.5~\mathsf{m g})$ , and MACl $(4.5~\mathsf{m g})$ in $7m L$ of a DMF:DMSO (4:1 v/v) solvent mixture. Then, $40~\upmu\up L$ of a CsI stock solution ( $1.5~\mathsf{M}$ in DMSO) was added to the precursor solution and the solution was left for overnight stirring in the glove box. The perovskite film was then deposited onto the different $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETLs using the one-step antisolvent technique. The green antisolvent used herein was prepared by mixing ethyl acetate (EA) and hexane (Hex) in $7{:}3\ \mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v},$ as reported in the authors’ previous work.[16,46] For the deposition of the perovskite film, $50~\upmu\up L$ of the perovskite precursor solution was spincoated onto the substrate using a two-stage spin-coating program, that is, 1500 rpm for $\rceil0\ s$ followed by 5500 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . At the $\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{10~s}}}$ interval during the second-stage spin-coating, ${\mathsf{100~}}\upmu\upiota$ of the EA–Hex mixed antisolvent was continuously dripped onto the spinning substrate. The perovskite films were then annealed at $45^{\circ}C$ for $2\mathsf{m i n}$ in the glovebox and a further $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $20{\mathsf{m i n}}$ in air. Spiro-OMeTAD was deposited onto the perovskite film by spin-coating $30~\upmu\up L$ of the mixed solution, which contained $72.3~\mathsf{m g}$ of spiro-OMeTAD salt in $1m L$ of CB, doped with $30~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $4{\cdot}t\mathsf{B}\mathsf{p}$ , and $35~\upmu\up L$ of Li-TSFI solution ( $260~\mathsf{m g}$ in $7m L$ of acetonitrile). Finally, an ${\approx}100~\mathsf{n m}~\mathsf{A g}$ contact was thermally evaporated as the top electrode using a shadow mask (aperture area: $0.09\ c m^{2}$ ; $1.75\ c m^{2})$ ). For the fabrication of large-area PSCs, the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ ETL $({\mathsf{S C}}.{\mathsf{S n O}}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{B i}{-}\mathsf{S n O}_{2};$ ) was first deposited on the patterned ITO substrate with dimensions of $5c m\times5c m$ , followed by the deposition of the perovskite film in the glovebox using the anti-solvent technique. A total of $400~\upmu\upiota$ of EA–Hex antisolvent was rapidly dripped onto the spinning substrate. A shadow mask (aperture area $\approx6.25~{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}]$ was used for Ag contact evaporation. Another smaller shadow mask (aperture area of $3.55c m^{2}$ ) was used for the $J-V$ measurement of the large-area PSCs.
Device Characterization: The surface morphology and topography of the different $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films and perovskite films were investigated using an FESEM (Hitachi SU-70) and an atomic force microscope (Park Systems NX10). The crystallographic changes of the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films were characterized by an $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffractometer (Bruker D8 Advance) using $\mathsf{C u K}\alpha$ excitation. The optical transmittance and absorption of the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ films were measured using a UV–vis spectrophotometer (PerkinElmer Lambda 750), and the steady-state photoluminescence spectra of the $S_{\mathsf{n O}_{2}}$ /perovskite samples were obtained from a photoluminescence spectrometer (Horiba FluoroMax-4) at an excitation wavelength of $460\ \mathsf{n m}$ . For PSC device characterization, the current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ measurements were conducted using a Keithley 2400 source meter under a simulated one-sun illumination ( $\mathsf{I O0\ m w c m^{-2}}$ ; AM 1.5G standard) from an Oriel Sol 3A solar simulator (Newport Inc.). Before conducting the measurements, the system was calibrated against a NREL-certified reference solar cell. The EQE spectra of the PSCs were acquired using an Oriel quantum efficiency measurement device (IQE-200). The TPV measurements were performed using a digital oscilloscope (Siglent, SDS 1302CFL, 300 MHz) and a function generator (Tektronix, AFG3022C) by setting the input impedance of oscilloscope to 1 MΩ. A white LED was used as biased light and a pulsed blue light $(473\ \mathsf{n m})$ ) generated by a laser diode (CNI laser, MBL-FN-473) was used as a perturbation source.[46,47] The background signal was set to zero by subtracting the average of the signal measured prior to excitation, and then the transient was fitted with a generic double exponential decay function. Dark impedance spectroscopy measurements were carried out using an impedance analyzer (CompactStat, IVIUM Tech) at voltage bias of $0.9\mathrm{V}$ and a perturbation voltage of $\mathsf{l}0\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}.$ The resulting Nyquist plots were fitted using ZView software (Scribner Associates).[48]
